Liberia caught between poverty and  progress in the interview Liberian  President Ellen Johnson Sirleaf your  excellency you are in Germany to receive  a prize for your efforts in rebuilding  Liberia you have been the president for  eight years now you've started numerous  initiatives to improve the education  sector to promote reconciliation to  promote economic growth you've come very  far but despite all your efforts  let birria still is a very poor a very  challenged country do you sometimes fall  into bed after a very long working day  as the president and wonder why am I  actually doing this to myself oh there  may be moments when when I wonder what  else could I do I never wonder why I  always wonder if I've not done enough if  we could all achieve at a much faster  pace so generally you know I'm very very  confident in the future and very  satisfied what we're doing even though  not at the pace I want to see what do  you think are your greatest achievements  so far as the president we've been able  to start the process of reconstruction  infrastructure building the  institution's getting the the laws and  the policies right settling our large  external debt to open up the fiscal  space getting kids back into school  health and educational systems and we've  had to tackle just about everything as a  priority ensuring that the environment  is one that promotes geoagro  democracy ensuring people have all the  freedoms starting all the reform process  restoring our international  relationships and so it's it's been a  challenging  situation but again as I said we're  pleased with the progress is there a  particular area where you are unhappy  with the progress that has been made so  far in our educational system because it  we have stated that it's our number one  priority the foundation upon which  everything has rests it's been difficult  because most of the professional  teachers had departed the country the  schools were broken down the everything  about it required reform and that has  contributed to the slow pace and to the  lag in our building the delay capacity  at the level that will ensure  satisfactory implementation of our  development plans when you came into  office you were greeted with a lot of  enthusiasm both locally and abroad and a  lot of people when you read the  commentaries in the media expected you  to kind of change the country overnight  to build a very stable prosperous  Liberia how do you cope with these kind  of enormous expectations that that  people have it's always difficult when  when people have been suppressed for a  long time and have not had opportunities  for a long time and all of a sudden  there's hope you know hope that comes  from from a new government and  enlightened government so managing those  expectations are very very challenging  endeavor but I must say that because  we've been able to make enough progress  to have given people hope and again have  given them a stake in society where they  to feel that the future belongs to them  because of that we've been able to have  10 consecutive years of peace and now we  think we can build upon that but still  there's no quick fix when a country has  been destroyed over two decades and all  institutions infrastructure systems have  been totally dysfunctional you can't fix  it in in 10 or 20 years even and how do  you cope with it personally  you just mentioned you understand the  needs of the people they are enormous  and Emily for instance if you travel the  country and people come they make  requests and you as the president of  course you know that you can't satisfy  all that demands immediately you know  that you first have to find the  necessary funds for instance how do you  cope with that personally well I guess  first you have to be truthful to the  people by letting them know there are  limitations in resources financial and  human in those cases where one can  respond to them in a small way one tries  to do that one has to also keep pointing  out that development is not just a a  government task or a leaders test if  element belongs to everybody and  everybody must make a contribution so  it's a continuous process of engaging  them and enlightening them and working  with them and you know despite the  challenges the message does get through  on the other hand you said it's  necessary to enlighten people of course  Liberia still faces challenges I mean  you've always spoken out very strongly  against corruption for instance do if  you read reports for instance by  Transparency International and there is  still a great deal of corruption does it  sometimes make you said that you are  trying to explain to people what is  necessary but you can still see that in  everyday life apparently for many people  it's still quite difficult to change  from the usual ways that's very true it  is one of the most challenging issues  that we have to face corruption became a  way of life as a means of survival and  so he became a part of the body culture  is systemic  it goes beyond government or any  individual group or person we've tried  to tackle it by putting in measures of  prevention and that means improving  people's compensation to reduce their  vulnerabilities coming up with the laws  and the policies rebuilding the  institutions rebuilding capacity we  still have some lags when it comes to  punishment because the system is still  part of an overall systemic  environment and so punishing people is  difficult in that situation we've been  changing laws and you know improving the  quality of our judges limiting bribery  and all of that and as a matter of fact  some progress has been made if you look  at the transparency international  indexes we've improved if you look at  the Millennium Challenge account indexes  we've also improved considerably but we  still have a long way to go how does it  make you feel if you have people like  for instance Lima and goo bovi who got  the Nobel Peace Prize together with you  who are now coming out to criticize you  does that hurt you because I mean you  know what you are doing it has to come  with leadership one has to be able to  take that as no escaping it I keep  saying that perhaps she's too young to  know the many struggles that we all went  through to bring the freedom that enable  her to be able to criticize and so that  that's part of what it what it takes to  be a leader and do you sometimes feel  that you have made any mistakes in  running the country oh yes I mean nobody  can say that you're perfect in in the  decisions you make so I've made some  mistakes maybe sometimes but not being  sufficiently hard on people who haven't  achieved the goals in a time I've wanted  what come from a small society where  everybody knows everybody and sometimes  that puts a lot of pressure on the  leader even if you want to be hard-nosed  and take those decisions so but but  generally you know accept the small  mistakes I think the bigger achievements  have certainly way outweighed the small  mistakes here and there  you earned a great deal of criticism  abroad but also locally for appointing  some of your family members to high  position of government now when you look  at it would you see that as a mistake no  in the realm of things many of them  earned a position before I became  president I saw no reason to remove them  if they were doing a good job the  capacity is limited in many  we're talking about at three  appointments sometimes we forget that  people who have the same name I have  rarely know relatives from some of them  have not even known all my life but in  those cases where they were to wreak  relative like sons and have served a  particular function I think they've  responded to specific needs that I had  and they can now move on as as one or  two of them are how satisfied are you  with the assistance from foreign donors  like Germany for instance in the  rebuilding of Liberia welfare police  with the response Germany has has worked  with us on our debt  Chancellor Merkel was particularly  instrumental in bringing the European  partners together the United States  remains our number one partner has  helped us to rebuild the army the  European Commission is a major partner  in infrastructure and that goes for our  multilateral partners like the World  Bank and the African Development Bank so  if there's anything that has slowed the  progress is our own capacity limitations  because we've not been able to negotiate  as as fast as we have wanted to or  implement at the aggressive pace that we  have wished for but we're quite pleased  with our partnership even our African  friends who have helped to keep the  peace what are the priorities for your  remaining years in office infrastructure  our power our ports our roads so that we  can develop the private sector which is  hurting our educational reform program  silver service reform we've been doing a  lot of work on that to make sure that we  get rid of the bloated public service  and we bring in the capacity  constitutional reform being able to to  do more on Liberian ization  diversification of the economy so that  we can move toward Agra processing and  industrialization and responding to the  big challenge of youth unemployment  giving our youth  skills and technical training so that  they too can take a much greater  participation in the economic life of  the country and continue to promote  democracy the freedoms that our people  enjoy which they have not had perhaps  all their lives today freedom of speech  Association religion we hope to continue  all of that and and build upon the  progress we've made consolidated and  make sure that I keep saying that our  2030 vision calls for being a  middle-income country by the year 2030  and it makes for years when I complete  my chairman our agenda for  transformation I hope will put it on an  irreversible course toward the  achievement of that long-term goal Madam  President thank you very much for the  interview thank you
 

